# The countryside's wider places (maps round 2, K5; docs/maps-plan.md): the Ford, Hollin Coppice, the Tarn, the
# Quarry and the Drovers' Inn, each with a recurring card in cycled showings (its placeholder retired) and scenes once
# each; and two counters, the country market in the market village and the hedge-witch's door at the Marsh. Hints of
# the valley's seventh year stay at the edge of sight: nothing here explains the custom (that is K4).

# ---------------- Counters ----------------

@event shop_country_market
title: The Country Market
place: home_market
type: shop
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 1
weight: 10
art: house
tags: money shop
scene: The back row of a village market under a stone market cross, a crook-maker splitting ash, a cloak-seller among hanging waxed cloaks, a bee-woman with combs under a veil, a girl at an oatcake griddle, a student browsing.
text: {~Behind the market cross, away from the green's stalls, is the row the valley actually shops at: a crook-maker splitting ash, a cloak-seller under a rack of waxed cloaks, the bee-woman in her veil with her combs, and a girl turning oatcakes on a griddle.|The back row again. The crook-maker is arguing with a shepherd about an inch of handle, the cloak-seller is asleep standing up, and the bee-woman has a bee on her nose and doesn't care.}
> honey: A comb of heather honey
  hint: Health and Mood.
  gold -tiny
  health +tiny
  mood +small
  say: {~The bee-woman cuts you a square of dark comb and wraps it in a dock leaf, for a coin. You eat it on the steps of the cross, wax and all.|The bee-woman takes your coin and lifts her veil to look at you before she sells. "You'll want the bitter one," she says. She's right.}
> oatcakes: Oatcakes off the griddle
  hint: Health; it ends a hungry month.
  gold -tiny
  health +small
  lose hungry
  say: {~A coin buys a stack of oatcakes hot off the griddle, with a knob of butter melting through. The girl turns the next batch with her fingers and doesn't burn them.|The oatcake girl takes your coin and gives you the broken ones too, free. "They taste the same," she says. They taste better.}
> cloak: A waxed cloak for the upland
  hint: A robe for fights: more Health, and beasts' blows hurt you less.
  when: not has_item(waxed_cloak)
  gold -medium
  give waxed_cloak
  say: {~You count out the coins for heavy waxed wool that smells of sheep and rain. The cloak-seller swears a man once slept a winter night on the ridge in it and woke up warm. He might have been the man.|The cloak-seller wakes up long enough to take your money. You put it on and the wind off the moor gives up trying.}
> crook: A shepherd's crook
  hint: A crook of seasoned ash: on the moors, one fall more you can survive.
  when: not has_item(shepherds_crook)
  gold -small
  give shepherds_crook
  say: {~You pay, and the crook-maker hands you one of seasoned ash with a horn handle. "For the bog roads," he says. "Feel ahead with it. The moor tells you where it's soft, if you ask with a stick."|He takes your coins, weighs you up and chooses a crook to match your height, then cuts an inch off it. "You'll stoop on the moor," he says. "Everybody does."}
> look: Watch the crook-maker work
  say: {~You watch the crook-maker steam an ash pole and bend its head round a form, slow as a clock hand. He doesn't speak, and you don't, and it's the most restful half hour of your break.|The shepherd and the crook-maker settle their inch at last. Neither of them is happy. They shake on it.}

@event shop_hedge_door
title: The Hedge-Witch's Door
place: countryside_marsh
type: shop
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 1
weight: 10
art: potion
tags: money shop magic
scene: A stilt house at the edge of a reed marsh, bunches of dried herbs hanging under its eaves, a lined old hedge-witch at a half-door with jars on the sill.
text: {has(origin_hedge): The hedge-witch's half-door, your grandmother's door, open over the reeds as it always is, with the jars on the sill and the rue drying above. Neighbours come up the plank all day for remedies, and pay in eggs, coin or news. "Same price for you," she says. "Family pays double."|The hedge-witch keeps a half-door over the reeds, open for anyone who comes up the plank, with jars on the sill and bunches of rue drying under the eaves. She sells remedies to the valley for coin, eggs or news, and serves a stranger without once meeting their eye.}
> rue: A bunch of rue for your pocket
  hint: Rue against the moor: on the moors, one fall more you can survive.
  when: has(origin_hedge) and not flag(marsh_rue)
  gold -tiny
  flag marsh_rue
  say: She ties the rue with red thread, the way she did when you were small, and puts it in your pocket herself, and takes your penny anyway. "Keep it by you on the high ground," she says. "Don't ask me why. You know why."
> poultice: A poultice for what hurts
  hint: Health, and it mends an injury.
  when: has(injured)
  gold -small
  health +small
  lose injured
  say: {~You pay for a poultice of comfrey and something green that smells of the bottom of a pond. It hurts worse for an hour, and then it doesn't hurt at all.|She takes your coin, binds it on herself, briskly, and tells you off for the injury as she does it.}
> rosemary: A sprig of rosemary that won't wilt
  hint: Health each month for a while.
  when: not has(hedge_sprig)
  gold -small
  gain hedge_sprig
  say: {~She takes your coin, snaps a sprig of rosemary from the pot by the door, breathes on it and gives it to you. "That won't wilt till it's done its work," she says. It doesn't.|A sprig of rosemary on a thread, for a coin. "For the road," she says, "and the road back."}
> look: Just pass the time of day
  say: {has(origin_hedge): You sit on the step with her for a while and say nothing, which is how you always talked.|You pass the time of day, and she passes it back, and the reeds talk between you.}

# ---------------- The Ford ----------------

@pool countryside_ford_life
place: countryside_ford
class: routine
weight: 10
tags: social

@event ford_crossing
title: The Ferryman's Toll
pool: countryside_ford_life
repeat: 3
tone: eerie
art: wave
scene: A wide shallow river ford with stepping stones, an old ferryman sitting by his flat punt on the bank, holding out a weathered hand to a student.
text: {&The river's up and the stepping stones are under it. The old ferryman will take you over in his punt, but he doesn't take coin. "Something odd," he says. "A button. A tune. Something you'd miss."|The river's low and the stones are dry, but the ferryman calls from his punt anyway. "Stones are slippery today," he says. "Toll's the same. Something odd."|Mist on the ford, the stones half-hidden. The ferryman holds out his hand without a word. On his wrist, threaded on a string, are a hundred odd tolls: buttons, bird skulls, a key, a tooth.}
> left: Pay his odd toll
  mood -tiny
  lore +tiny
  say: {&You give him a button off your coat. He threads it on his string with a hundred others and poles you over, and when you look back he's sitting on the far bank again, which is the side you started from.|You hum him a tune you made up as a child. He listens carefully, nods and poles you over humming it. You can't remember it afterwards. It's gone.|You give him a hair ribbon you were fond of. He takes it gravely, ties it on, and the punt crosses through the mist without a sound.}
> right: Cross on the stones
  challenge feat apprentice:
    win:
      vigor +small
      say: {&You go over the drowned stones by feel, knee-deep, cold to the bone and laughing. The ferryman watches you all the way and looks, if anything, disappointed.|Stone to stone, quick and dry-shod. The ferryman claps, slowly, from his punt.|You cross in the mist by counting stones: nine. Coming back at dusk, you count them again: eight. You don't think about it.}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {&The third stone rolls and you go in to the waist. The river is very cold and very interested in your boots. The ferryman fishes you out with his pole, and doesn't charge.|You slip on the slick stone and sit down hard in the shallows. The ferryman doesn't laugh, which is worse.|In the mist you miss a stone entirely and go in to the chest. You come out shaking, on the bank you started from.}

@event ford_spate_cart
title: The River in Spate
pool: countryside_ford_life
tone: tense
time: morning noon afternoon
art: wave
scene: A river ford in brown flood, a farm cart stuck mid-stream with its horse panicking in the traces, a farmer on the bank shouting, a student wading in.
text: After a night of rain on the upland the ford is in spate, brown and fast, and a carter has tried it anyway. His cart is stuck mid-stream, the water's up to the axles and rising, and the horse is panicking in the traces. The carter's on the bank, shouting at the horse, the river and you, in that order.
> left: Wade in and calm the horse
  challenge feat journeyman:
    win:
      alignment +small
      vigor +tiny
      say: You get to the horse's head with the water at your chest and talk it down, slowly, nose to nose, until it'll walk. Horse, cart and you come out together onto the far bank. The carter gives you a cheese, a whole one, and tells everybody.
    lose:
      health -small
      say: The current takes your feet and you go under once, twice, and grab the cart's wheel. The ferryman gets the horse out with his pole and you out with the other end. The cart's lost downriver. So, very nearly, were you.
> right: Free the traces with a spell
  challenge spell apprentice:
    win:
      arcana +small
      gold +tiny
      say: You cut the traces from the bank with a snap of your wand, and the horse surges out free. The cart goes off downriver towards the mill with a cargo of turnips. The carter is so glad about the horse he pays you anyway.
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: Your spell cuts the wrong strap, and the cart tips. Horse and carter are fine. The turnips are now the river's. The carter tells you exactly what he thinks of the academy.

@event ford_ferryman_side
title: Which Side
pool: countryside_ford_life
tone: eerie
time: afternoon evening
when: seen(ford_crossing) > 0
art: wave
scene: A quiet river ford at evening, an old ferryman in his punt mid-stream not moving, a student on the bank calling to him.
text: You've noticed something about the old ferryman: he takes people over and back all day, and he never gets out of the punt, on either side. Not to eat. Not for anything. You ask him, half joking, which bank is his. He looks at you for a long moment, the punt quite still in the current. "Whichever's the other one," he says.
> left: Ask him what he means
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: He tells you he crossed once, years ago, the wrong way, and it's easier to stay on the water. Then he poles away downstream and doesn't come back for an hour. You've learned something, and you don't know what. You're cold all evening.
> right: Bring him a pie from the market
  alignment +tiny
  gold -tiny
  say: You bring him a hot pie and hand it out to the punt. He eats it on the water, slowly, watching both banks. "Kind," he says. "You're kind. Mind the seventh step on the stones." Then he won't say anything else.

# ---------------- Hollin Coppice ----------------

@pool hollin_coppice_life
place: hollin_coppice
class: routine
weight: 10
tags: nature

@event coppice_rides
title: Along the Rides
pool: hollin_coppice_life
repeat: 3
tone: warm
art: tree
scene: A sunlit coppice wood of straight young hazel and ash poles growing from old stools, grassy rides between them, a student with a basket among ferns and fungi.
text: {&Hollin Coppice is all rides and clearings, straight young poles from stumps older than the villages, and the floor of it is a larder if you know where to look.|In the coppice the charcoal burners are cutting this year's panel, and the rides are full of the sound of billhooks and the smell of sap.|The coppice in a soft rain, every leaf dripping, the rides empty except for a woodpecker and you.}
> left: Forage along the rides
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: {&{season == "winter": Catkins on the hazels, a ring of velvet shank on a dead stool and a patch of something your herbal calls "use with care". You come home scratched to the elbows with a basket worth a week of lessons.|Wood sorrel, the first hazelnuts, a ring of chanterelles and a patch of something your herbal calls "use with care". You come home scratched to the elbows with a basket worth a week of lessons.}|Among the cut stools you find birch polypore and a clump of comfrey the burners don't want. The brambles take their toll on your arms.|Rain brings the mushrooms up. You fill your basket, and your boots fill with water.}
> right: Help the charcoal burners
  gold +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {&You stack cordwood for the burners' mound all afternoon, black to the eyebrows. They pay in coin and sausage, and tell you the mound must never be left unwatched, not for a minute, not ever.|You help bundle the cut poles. The burners pay you and sing while they work, a song with seven verses and no chorus. Your shoulders are raw.|The burners need their mound turfed before the rain gets in. You turf it, wet through, and they pay you double for the weather.}

@event coppice_woodward
title: The Woodward's Marks
pool: hollin_coppice_life
tone: eerie
art: tree
scene: A coppice ride with a tall stooped man in an old green coat chalking a mark on a tree trunk, a student watching from behind a stool of hazel.
text: Down a far ride you finally see who chalks the marks on every seventh tree: a tall, stooped man in a green coat so old it's grey, who moves from trunk to trunk with a lump of chalk and doesn't hurry. The burners say nobody hired him and nobody remembers him starting. He sees you, and holds the chalk out.
> left: Take the chalk and mark the next one
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: You count seven trees and chalk the seventh. He nods, takes the chalk back and walks on into the wood, and you can't see where he goes. All the way home you count things in sevens without meaning to: steps, fence posts, crows.
> right: Ask him who he works for
  charm +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: "The wood," he says, as if it were obvious, and goes on chalking. You ask what the marks are for. "Counting," he says. "Somebody has to." When you look again the ride is empty and the chalk is wet on a tree you'd swear he never reached.

@event coppice_charcoal_night
title: Watching the Mound
pool: hollin_coppice_life
time: evening
tone: warm
art: flame
scene: A charcoal burners' camp in a wood clearing at night, a smoking turf-covered mound glowing in the dark, two burners at a small fire inviting a student to sit with them.
text: A charcoal mound has to be watched all night, every night of the burn, or it flares and the whole charge is ash. The two burners on tonight have a pot on the fire and a spare log to sit on, and they're glad of a third. "Stay and watch," says the older one. "We'll tell you about the wood."
> left: Sit up all night with them
  lore +small
  health -small
  say: You sit up with them till dawn, poking the mound when it smokes wrong, while they tell you about the wood: the stools older than the villages, a winter when the mound went out and nobody spoke of it, the woodward who chalks the trees. You're stiff and smoked like a ham by morning.
> right: Share their supper and go
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: You put a coin in for the pot, and they give you rabbit stew and three good stories, and send you home before the dark comes down properly. {at_home(): "Mind the dark on the way," says the older one. "You know the wood. Doesn't mean it knows you."|"Not a place to walk late," says the older one, "for folk who aren't from here."}

# ---------------- The Tarn ----------------

@pool countryside_tarn_life
place: countryside_tarn
class: routine
weight: 10
tags: nature

@event tarn_still_water
title: The Still Water
pool: countryside_tarn_life
repeat: 3
tone: eerie
art: moon
scene: A small black mountain lake in a cup of rock under a crag, so still the sky is mirrored in it, a student sitting at the edge looking down into the water.
text: {&The tarn lies up on the upland in a cup of stone, black and so still that the clouds in it look deeper than the clouds above. Over it stands a crag, and on the crag a cairn somebody keeps building.|The tarn is ruffled today, wind chasing cat's-paws across it, and the water's the colour of strong tea at the edge and black in the middle. The crag above is loud with wind.|{season == "winter": The tarn is frozen from shore to shore, black ice so clear you can see weed standing in it like a forest, and the crag above it is white.|The tarn on a hot day, still as glass, a dragonfly the only thing moving, the crag above it shimmering.}}
> left: Climb the crag above it
  challenge feat apprentice+:
    win:
      vigor +small
      mood +small
      say: {&You climb the crag hand over hand to the cairn at the top and put a stone on it, as everyone does. From up here the tarn is a black eye looking at the sky.|You climb into the wind and it tries to pluck you off the crag, and doesn't. At the top you shout something, and the wind takes it away over the valley.|{season == "winter": You climb the white crag with frozen fingers and stand by the cairn over the frozen tarn, the whole upland ringing with cold.|You climb the warm rock to the cairn and lie on top of the crag in the sun like a lizard.}}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {&A hold crumbles and you slide down the crag on your front, scraping both knees. The tarn watches, perfectly still.|The wind takes you off balance on the last pitch and you come down the scree in a hurry, bruised.|{season == "winter": The ice on the crag is harder than it looks, and so is the ground at the bottom.|The rock is hot enough to burn your hands, and you let go of the wrong one.}}
> right: Sit and look into the water
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {&You sit at the edge and look into the tarn until the clouds in it seem to be the real ones and the sky above a picture of them. The cold comes up out of the stone into you, and you walk down stiff.|You watch the wind write on the water and rub it out, write and rub out, for an hour, in a wind that goes straight through your coat.|{season == "winter": You lie on the black ice and look down at the weed forest standing under you, and something long and silver turns among it, and is gone. You get up shaking with cold.|You look into the still water and see yourself and the sky behind you, and behind the sky, deeper, more sky. You come down sunburnt.}}

@event tarn_angler
title: The Angler at the Tarn
pool: countryside_tarn_life
tone: warm
art: wave
scene: An old angler in a tweed hat sitting on a folding stool at the edge of a black mountain lake, a long rod over the water, a student sitting down beside him.
text: An old angler is sitting on a folding stool at the tarn's edge, as he has every fine day, the shepherds tell you, for forty years. He's fishing for a trout that lives in the black middle and that nobody but him has ever seen. "She's there," he says, not looking round. "She's just choosy. Sit, if you'll sit quiet."
> left: Sit quiet with him all day
  lore +tiny
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: You sit quiet with him all day in the wind. He doesn't catch her. Near sunset the line goes taut once, hard, and slack again, and he laughs out loud. "There," he says. "Told you." It's the best thing that happens all week. You're chilled to the bone.
> right: Offer to charm the line
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: You offer a little charm on the hook. He looks at you as if you'd spat in the tarn. "Then it wouldn't be her choosing," he says, and you sit in silence, the charm unspoken, and learn something about patience that no lecture covered.

# ---------------- The Quarry ----------------

@pool countryside_quarry_life
place: countryside_quarry
class: routine
weight: 10
tags: nature

@event quarry_face
title: The Quarry Face
pool: countryside_quarry_life
repeat: 3
tone: light
art: rune
scene: An old stone quarry with tall pale rock faces full of fossil shells, ledges and a rope left by climbers, a student testing a handhold.
text: {&The old quarry's walls are full of fossils, shells from a sea that left before the valley had a name. A frayed old rope hangs down the tallest face, left by somebody braver, and the ledges are thick with the best of the shells.|The quarry after rain: every face streaming, every fossil shining, the rope dark with wet.|{season == "winter": The quarry in a hard bright frost, the faces rimed white and the shells standing out like carvings.|The quarry in high summer, the pale faces throwing the heat back at you and the shells too hot to touch.}}
> left: Climb the face
  challenge feat journeyman:
    win:
      vigor +small
      lore +tiny
      say: {&You climb to the top ledge on the old rope and your own fingers, and find a spiral shell as big as your hand, perfect, waiting. You climb down with it in your teeth.|You climb the wet face, slowly, every hold slick, and come down muddy and triumphant with a fossil fern.|{season == "winter": You climb the frosty face with numb fingers and find, on the top ledge, a sea-urchin turned to stone, sitting there like an egg in a nest.|You climb the hot face with chalky fingers and find, on the top ledge, a sea-urchin turned to stone, sitting there like an egg in a nest.}}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {&The old rope gives a yard of slack and you slide down the face, skinning both palms. The quarry's echo says something sarcastic.|Halfway up, a wet hold comes away in your hand. You land on the scree heap at the bottom, bruised and furious.|{season == "winter": The frost on the ledge is ice. You come down faster than you went up.|Your sweating fingers slip on the hot rock. You come down faster than you went up.}}
> right: Hunt the scree for fossils
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {&You turn over scree for an hour and find a dozen broken shells and one whole one, small and perfect. Your back aches from the stooping.|You sift the wet scree and find a tooth, a real tooth, from something that swam. Your hands are cold and gritty.|{season == "winter": You find nothing in the frozen scree for an hour, and then a little curled ammonite, which is enough. Your fingers have stopped working.|You find nothing in the baking scree for an hour, and then a little curled ammonite, which is enough. The back of your neck is burnt.}}

@event quarry_echo
title: Back Chat
pool: countryside_quarry_life
tone: light
art: rune
scene: The floor of an old quarry, a student with hands cupped to their mouth shouting at a pale rock face, a shepherd boy on the rim laughing.
text: The valley says the quarry's echo is full of opinions, and a shepherd boy on the rim dares you to try it. "Good morning," you call. "Is it?" says the quarry. You call that you're from the academy. "Oh," says the quarry, "one of those." The shepherd boy is lying on the grass, helpless.
> left: Argue with it
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: You argue with the quarry for half an hour, about the weather, the academy and whether it has any right to an opinion. It gives as good as it gets, in your own voice, and gets the last word, every time. The boy calls it a draw. You're hoarse, and somehow lost.
> right: Ask it a real question
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: You ask it what it's seen. There's a long pause, the only one all morning. "Sea," says the quarry at last, "and then not." Then it goes back to mocking your accent. You sit on the scree a long time, feeling very small and very recent.

@event quarry_millstone
title: The Last Millstone
pool: countryside_quarry_life
tone: warm
time: morning noon afternoon
art: house
scene: A quarry floor, two old stonecutters with chisels finishing a great round millstone, a student helping lever it with a long bar.
text: Two old stonecutters are finishing a millstone on the quarry floor, the last, they say, this quarry will ever cut: the mill's old stone is worn smooth after forty years, and nobody young wants the trade. It has to be levered onto a sledge before the miller's cart comes. They look at you, and at your arms, and hand you a bar.
> left: Put your back into it
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: The three of you lever the great stone up inch by inch with bars and wedges, and when it settles on the sledge both old men sit down on it and laugh. They give you a chip of the stone to keep, "for luck at the mill". Your back will remember it for days.
> right: Float it with a spell
  challenge spell apprentice:
    win:
      arcana +small
      mood +small
      say: The stone rises a foot off the ground, turns slowly like a coin, and settles on the sledge as gently as a cake on a plate. The stonecutters say nothing for a long time. Then the elder says, "Well, that's the trade finished, then," and shakes your hand.
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: The stone lifts, wobbles and comes down on the bar, which springs up and catches your shin. The stonecutters lever it on the old way. They're kind about it. Your shin isn't.

# ---------------- The Drovers' Inn ----------------

@pool drovers_inn_life
place: drovers_inn
class: routine
weight: 10
tags: social money

@event drovers_long_table
title: The Long Table
pool: drovers_inn_life
repeat: 3
time: evening night
tone: light
art: cup
scene: A rough country inn with one long table packed with drovers, tinkers and travellers, a big fire, tankards, a student squeezing onto the bench.
text: {&The Drovers' Inn has one long table, one fire and one rule, which nobody can remember. Drovers, tinkers and a man with a bear on a rope share it with whoever comes in.|Market night at the Drovers'. The long table is three to a seat, the fire roars and two drovers are arm-wrestling for the last of the stew.|A wet night at the Drovers'. Steam rises off every coat at the long table, and a tinker by the fire is singing a song with far too many verses.}
> left: Squeeze onto the bench
  gold -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {&You buy a round of the cheapest beer in the valley, which tastes of it, and in return get the whole history of the bear. The bear sleeps through it.|You pay for a bowl of stew and sit between two drovers who argue across you about the price of wethers. By the end you're arguing too.|You buy the singer a pint to wet the next verse, join the chorus by the third and lead it by the seventh.}
> right: Arm-wrestle a drover
  challenge feat journeyman:
    win:
      renown +tiny
      mood +small
      say: {&Elbow on the table, hand in a hand like a saddle. You hold, and hold, and put him down, and the long table roars. The bear wakes up and roars too.|You win the last of the stew, and it's terrible, and you eat every mouthful in front of the whole room.|You beat the champion of the long table and he buys you a drink, and then wants a rematch, and then another drink.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {&He puts your hand down on the table like a man closing a gate. Everyone's very nice about it. Your wrist isn't.|You lose the stew and very nearly your wrist.|You lose so fast the room doesn't notice. Your shoulder does.}

@event drovers_tinker
title: The Tinker's Pack
pool: drovers_inn_life
tone: light
art: gear
scene: A tinker by an inn fire with his pack open on the floor, pots, knives and odd brass trinkets spread around him, holding up a bent spoon to a student.
text: A tinker by the fire has his pack open on the hearth: pots, knives, scissors, a box of odd brass trinkets, and a sign that says MENDS ANYTHING. He's looking at the things you carry with a professional eye. "That's bent," he says, of something you hadn't noticed was. "I'll mend it. Or I'll trade you a luck for it."
> left: Mend his pots while he talks
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  say: You sit by the fire and help him mend a drover's kettle, burning your fingers on the solder, while he tells you about every inn on the high road from here to the sea. He fixes your bag strap for nothing, in return. You know the high road now, by heart.
> right: Trade for one of his lucks
  guile +small
  gold -tiny
  say: You haggle him for a "luck", a brass horse no bigger than your thumb. It isn't magic. You check, twice. But you learn more about bargaining from the tinker's patter than from a year of market days, and you suspect he cheated you very fairly.

@event drovers_loose_bear
title: The Bear Gets Loose
pool: drovers_inn_life
tone: tense
time: evening night
art: paw
scene: A rough country inn's taproom in uproar, a big brown bear loose among the tables with a broken rope trailing, drovers climbing onto benches, a student standing very still with a wand lowered.
text: The man with the bear has fallen asleep by the fire, and the bear's rope has come undone, and the bear has decided to see what's in the kitchen. The long table empties onto the benches. The landlord is up on the bar with a ladle. The bear stops in the middle of the room and looks at you, because you're the only one not shouting.
> left: Talk the bear down
  challenge soothe journeyman:
    win:
      renown +small
      mood +small
      say: You hold out a hand and talk to it, low and steady, about nothing, the way you'd talk to a frightened horse. The bear listens, sighs like a bellows and sits down. You walk it back to its sleeping owner by the broken rope. The Drovers' Inn will tell this story for years.
    lose:
      health -small
      say: The bear listens to about half of what you say, then shoves past you towards the kitchen, and you go over a bench backwards. It finds the cheese. The owner wakes up and fetches it out by the ear, apologising to everyone, especially you.
> right: Lure it out with the stew pot
  gold -tiny
  charm +tiny
  say: You buy the rest of the stew, all of it, and carry the steaming pot out into the yard, and the bear follows you like a dog. It eats the lot under the stars while the drovers cheer from the doorway. You smell of mutton for two days.
